  '23': [{"type": "p", "text": "Your patient data is increasingly scattered across multiple portals, and every portal they use means another data silos your team has to integrate. In 2024, 59% of individuals had multiple online portals or medical records — up from just 24% in 2020. That's not a user experience problem anymore. It's an integration infrastructure problem."}, {"type": "h2", "text": "The Multi-Portal Reality"}, {"type": "p", "text": "For decades, most patients worked with a single health system. They logged into one portal, they accessed their records from one place. Simple. But healthcare has fragmented. Patients now visit urgent care, specialists, hospital systems, retail clinics, and home health providers — each with its own portal. Some patients have Medicare, commercial insurance, and Medicaid simultaneously. The result is a patchwork: 59% of individuals in 2024 now actively manage multiple portals to see their full health picture."}, {"type": "p", "text": "What this means operationally: your EHR team can no longer assume that the medical record you maintain is the complete record your patient sees. Patients are aggregating data themselves through third-party apps, or more likely, they are not aggregating it at all and making decisions based on incomplete information. That's a clinical risk. It is also a competitive disadvantage: if your patients cannot easily see their full history, they will find a system that lets them."}, {"type": "p", "text": "Figure 1: Patient portal access fragmentation (2020-2024). Single-portal users declined from 76% to 41%, while multi-portal users rose from 24% to 59%."}, {"type": "h2", "text": "Why Data Aggregation Is an Interfacing Problem"}, {"type": "p", "text": "In the old portal era, interfacing meant connecting your EHR to a patient portal. One connection, one data direction, one use case. Today, interfacing means:"}, {"type": "li", "text": "Ingesting data from multiple external sources (patient-reported, insurance-provided, lab uploads, wearable integrations)."}, {"type": "li", "text": "Standardizing that data so your clinical workflows can use it."}, {"type": "li", "text": "Managing consent and privacy rules that vary by source."}, {"type": "li", "text": "Creating a single logical view of the patient that your teams and your patients can trust."}, {"type": "p", "text": "None of this is true portal magic. It is a data management problem. You need ETL (extract, transform, load) logic. You need master data management (MDM) or at least a data governance layer. You need APIs that can both send and receive data securely and at scale."}, {"type": "p", "text": "The challenge is acute for mid-market organizations. Large health systems often have enough IT budget and data engineering capacity to build custom aggregation pipelines. Mid-market organizations often do not. They have a portal contract with their EHR vendor, maybe a few third-party API integrations, but no coherent strategy for ingesting, normalizing, and presenting patient data from multiple sources."}, {"type": "h2", "text": "Identity Stitching: The Hidden Complexity"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Behind data aggregation sits a harder problem: identity stitching. When your patient is Mary Johnson at Hospital A, Mary M. Johnson at Urgent Care B, and MAJ at Insurance C, your systems need to confidently determine that these are the same person. Do they match on name and date of birth? What about middle initial? What if there are spelling variations or the patient changed names?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Get this wrong and you either duplicate records (creating duplicate work and risking duplicate testing) or you miss records entirely (creating safety gaps). Get it right and you need a deterministic, explainable algorithm that your teams trust — and that regulators will accept as defensible if something goes wrong."}, {"type": "p", "text": "This is where many organizations stumble. They implement FHIR APIs and data exchange standards, but they skip the identity reconciliation layer. Six months later, they have a warehouse full of patient data that they cannot confidently use because the identity layer is too weak."}, {"type": "h2", "text": "What This Means for Your IT and Integration Strategy"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Start with a data governance foundation. You cannot build a safe multi-source data aggregation layer on top of sloppy naming conventions or undefined data ownership. Ask your teams:"}, {"type": "li", "text": "Do we have a single source of truth for patient identity? Or are we managing multiple, disconnected patient master files?"}, {"type": "li", "text": "Can we reliably ingest and map data from external sources? Or do we still rely on manual data entry for anything outside our EHR?"}, {"type": "li", "text": "Do we have a documented consent framework that lets us ingest, use, and share patient data from different sources? Or are we guessing at regulatory requirements?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "If your answers are shaky, you are not alone. AHIMA's workforce research found that 66% of health information professionals reported staffing shortages, which makes it hard to build and maintain governance infrastructure. The smarter path for mid-market organizations is to work with vendors and integration partners that have already solved these problems at scale — not to build them from scratch."}, {"type": "h2", "text": "The Tools and Standards You Will Need"}, {"type": "p", "text": "FHIR, HL7 v2, Direct Protocol, and API standards are the table stakes. But they are not sufficient. You will also need:"}, {"type": "li", "text": "Master Data Management (MDM) or a comparable patient identity reconciliation layer — either through your EHR vendor, a dedicated MDM tool, or a healthcare-specific data integration platform."}, {"type": "li", "text": "Consent management infrastructure that lets you tag, enforce, and audit consent rules as data flows through your system."}, {"type": "li", "text": "A data warehouse or analytics platform that can normalize patient data from multiple sources and expose it through APIs or reports without creating compliance risk."}, {"type": "li", "text": "Audit and logging infrastructure that can prove to regulators exactly where every piece of patient data came from, how it was used, and who accessed it."}, {"type": "p", "text": "Bottom line: The 59% of patients with multiple portals are not waiting for your organization to build a unified view of their health. They are building it themselves, or they are not building it at all. Either way, your system is a step behind. Data aggregation and identity stitching are no longer nice-to-have IT projects. They are the core infrastructure that separates compliant, competitive health systems from those that are stuck managing disparate portals and duplicate records."}, {"type": "p", "text": "Sources"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Source: ASTP Patient Access to Health Information Data Brief — healthit.gov"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Source: AHIMA Workforce and Burnout Research — ahima.org"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Source: CMS-0057-F Interoperability Rule — cms.gov"}],
